00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:37.820
product Okay,

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:40.920
but for our purposes we just
want one of the vectors that's

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.360
perpendicular to that point And
so in this case, yeah We could

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:47.260
use the cross product and it
would tell us a vector and tell

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:49.720
me what what vector did the
cross product give You I'm gonna

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:53.220
give me zero zero five.
It gave you zero zero something

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:53.980
Okay,

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:57.200
and so if the cross product
gives you back zero zero five,

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.860
what's that what that telling
you?

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:02.500
or what that's telling you.

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:05.260
And in the next example, we will
actually work it out.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:08.440
But suppose you found that the
cross product is 0, 0, 5.

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:12.200
What that's telling you is that
the equation of this plane is 0x

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:18.140
plus 0y plus 5z equals what?

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:21.060
0.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:22.220
0, good.

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:23.820
But if I simplify this a little,

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:28.020
the 0x and 0y can go away.
And I'm really just left with 5z

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.520
equals 0.

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:33.270
But then I'm really just left
with z equals 0.

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:36.660
So that is a perfectly valid
equation of a plane.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:41.540
And if we consult our friend
Desmos over here,

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:48.380
and we type in z equals 0,

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:53.220
that's a perfectly fine plate.
It's a set of all vectors in R3

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:58.040
whose z component is 0.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:03.260
Now, knowing that that's what
the answer ends up being,

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:05.080
can you think of a way we could
have done this without the cross

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:06.080
product?

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:08.080
Yeah,

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:08.520
Alex?

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:12.340
Because both z components of the
vector is 0, we know that the

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:15.520
span is trapped on the xy plane.
Exactly.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:17.320
Exactly.
Now, if we look at the two

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.680
vectors that we were given, 3,
2, 0, negative 1, 1, 0,

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:23.280
we know that both of them are
trapped on the xy plane.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:27.380
They both had zeros in that
third component.

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:31.420
So knowing that we know that all
you need are two vectors in R3

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.700
to define a plane,

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:36.700
what that tells you is that all
linear combinations of these two

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:37.820
vectors at the top,

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:40.120
3, 2, 0, and negative 1, 1, 0,

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.460
are forced to have a z value of
0.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:45.380
So the plane that they span is
just all vectors with a z value

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:46.880
of 0.

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:55.960
What about the second one,
three, two, four,

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:56.780
negative one,

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:57.120
one,

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:58.120
two?

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:09.880
How might we proceed here?

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.640
First I'll note that the cross
product is not the only way to

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:13.440
proceed.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.220
Remember what we are looking
for,

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:17.560
if you go back to that picture,

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:22.810
if this is the plane spanned by
these two vectors,

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:27.280
we're looking for a vector,
which we might call the normal

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:28.280
vector.

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:30.940
Normal meaning perpendicular
here.

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:36.190
That is orthogonal to both 3, 2,
4 and negative 1, 1, 2.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:38.680
You don't need to know anything
about a cross product to find

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:39.940
that.
It's just that the cross product

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:43.580
is like a surefire formula that
tells you one possible normal

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:44.500
vector.

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.560
But how might you do this
otherwise?

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.060
You could set up a system of
equations.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:51.540
You can say, pretend that normal
vector is ABC.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:55.290
I'll write that term here,
normal.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:58.180
Pretend that vector is ABC.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:02.580
Well, then the dot product of
ABC with three, two, four

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:03.940
should be zero.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:07.160
Now, let me just annotate here.
This is just three,

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:10.910
two, four dotted with ABC.

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:16.020
And then the dot product of ABC
with negative one,

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:17.020
one,

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:19.200
two should also be zero.

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:22.820
And what you could do is just
find one combination of A, B,

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:26.570
and C that satisfy this.

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:29.680
Of course, there will be
infinitely many,

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:32.740
and that corresponds to the fact
that there are infinitely many

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:35.800
vectors that are perpendicular
to this plane.

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:41.660
So, we should expect that there
are infinitely many solutions

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:45.410
for A, B, and C.

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:12.000
OK, so you can play around with
these equations,

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:15.240
and you can find a combination
of A, B, and C that satisfy

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:15.920
this.

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:18.300
That's a perfectly valid way to
do it.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:21.160
The cross product just tells you
one of them.

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:24.260
It tells you a specific one that
has some geometric

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:26.960
interpretation beyond the fact
that it's perpendicular to both

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:28.960
3, 2, 4, and negative 1, 1, 2.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:31.440
But we're not going to care much
about the length of the cross

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:32.420
product here.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:35.920
We're just going to use it as
being a tool that tells us one

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.920
of the many vectors
perpendicular to both of these.

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:53.460
OK.

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:54.960
Can someone?

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.780
I'm going to erase this picture
up here,

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:00.160
because it's the same picture
from before, though I should go

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:04.840
back in here, say, this is a
normal vector,

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:07.680
just so the pictures are there.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:11.520
Can someone tell me how you
would find the cross product

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:14.520
of 3, 2, 4?

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:17.800
It's written with the regular
multiplication symbol,

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:19.660
negative 1, 1, 2.

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:24.160
What does that end up being?

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:26.240
Sorry.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:29.880
It's all right.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:31.580
You want the final answer?

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:33.840
Tell me how you got it.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:36.160
I write like i, j,

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:36.880
k,

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:41.860
and then I do like 3, 2, 4,
negative 1, 1, 2, and then put a

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:45.680
pencil over the i and do the
determinant of the other four

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:46.680
components.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:49.880
So that is one way you can do
it, but that requires knowing

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:52.280
something about a determinant,
which we'll only cover in like a

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:55.060
month or something.
But the general idea that you

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:57.440
just said holds true as well
here

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:03.160
you start with the second
component and you do a 2 times

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:06.640
b 3 or let's say u 2 times v 3

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:09.480
Minus these two things
multiplied so it would be 2

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:13.980
times 2 minus 1 times 4.
What does that give you?

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:15.780
0

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:20.680
Then you do the next pair 4
times negative 1

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:24.560
minus 3 times 2 What does that
give you?

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:26.060
Negative 10.

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:28.180
And then lastly,

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:32.080
3 times 1 minus 2 times negative
1.

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:33.800
What does that give you?

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:37.550
3 minus negative 2, that's
positive 5.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:40.560
So what that tells you is the
plane equation

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:46.360
is 0x minus 10y plus 5z equals
0.

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.020
That's where these numbers are
coming from.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:55.520
But you can simplify this a
little to what?

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:04.340
What's the nicer way to write
this?

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:07.140
Yeah.

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:09.980
0, negative 2, 1.

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:10.800
Good.

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:14.020
I could notice that all I'm
really looking for is one vector

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:16.360
that's perpendicular to both.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:20.740
And it looks nicest if the
numbers in the equation are as

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:22.200
small as they need to be.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:25.960
So what I could do is just
divide both sides of this

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:27.620
equation by negative 5.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.880
And what that would tell you is
that the equation of the plane

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:35.060
is also 2y minus z equals 0.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:38.240
And that can't really be further
simplified.

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:43.620
So this is the set of all
vectors whose z component

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:46.860
is double their y component in
R3.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:47.960
That's all this is really
saying.

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:52.460
Right? This says y equals 2z.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:56.400
We should double check that the
vectors we started with satisfy

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:57.400
this.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.780
Does 3, 2, 4 satisfy that?

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:00.320
Yeah, sure.

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:01.880
4 is double 2.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.780
And does negative 1, 1, 2
satisfy that? Sure, 2 is double

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:05.400
1.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:08.120
So that means both vectors
satisfy this equation.

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:11.520
That means we found the right
plane equation.

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:12.160
OK?

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:14.400
The two examples we've worked
through

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:17.920
ended up giving us rather simple
looking equations, like z equals

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:21.160
0 and 2y minus z equals 0.

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:22.420
But in general,

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:25.960
if this was a 1 that we got back
in the cross product,

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:29.100
then the equation would have
been x minus 10y plus 5z equals

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:31.760
0, and that's a perfectly valid
equation.

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:34.600
The bigger picture here is that
the cross product tells us a way

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:37.820
of finding one of these normal
vectors,

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:40.600
but don't rely on that as the
only way.

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:43.120
Sometimes there is information
in the question or the situation

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:47.120
that tells you more naturally a
vector that is perpendicular to

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:48.280
the spanning vectors.

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:49.020
Like here,

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:50.360
you don't need to use the cross
product.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:53.640
You can just look at this and
tell that 0, 0, 1 would be

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:56.220
orthogonal to both of these
vectors.

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.880
Or if you look at old exam
questions,

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:00.540
there's one that says,

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:03.940
here are three vectors that are
all perpendicular to each other

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:05.440
in R3.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:09.920
Find the equation of the plane
spanned by the first two.

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:11.980
And I remember on that question,
a lot of people took the cross

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:12.780
product.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.600
But the cross product just told
you that it was equal.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:16.900
like the cross product was just
the third vector.

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:19.400
Because we told you all three of
the vectors were perpendicular

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:20.400
to each other.

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:22.140
So if I tell you that, then you
don't need to take the cross

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:24.380
product of these two.
You can just use the third one

00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:25.620
that I gave you.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.580
That's the kind of thinking I
want you to use here.

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:31.120
By the way, the cross product,
the reason I'm not covering it

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:35.000
in depth in class is because
it's a very specific thing that

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.180
only works for two vectors in
R3.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:41.860
Because what it tells you is the
direction that is perpendicular

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:44.580
to both of them.
But that's only a thing that

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.120
works in R3.

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:48.780
Imagine we're thinking in R4,
where there's like four

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:51.780
independent directions in which
you can move.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:55.480
There's no one direction that is
perpendicular to the span of two

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:56.260
vectors.

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:58.580
There's lots of directions that
are perpendicular to the span of

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:00.080
two vectors.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:02.760
And so the cross product, it's
like a very specific thing.

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.020
It's only for two vectors in R3.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:08.240
The dot product, on the other
hand, works for two vectors in

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:09.220
any universe.

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:10.560
And it's more general,

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:14.100
which is why we spend much more
time talking about it.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.850
Let me emphasize that here.

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:33.650
Questions with that so far?

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:35.500
Yeah,

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:36.520
Gavin and then Paul.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:38.120
Oh, me first? Yeah, sure.
OK.

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:42.620
So we were saying that the
equation for a plane

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:45.960
is when the dot product equals
0.

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:48.040
Why is that the case?

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:49.040
Well,

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:52.360
if we go back to here,

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:56.800
first, we reasoned about why the
d here has to be 0.

00:24:56.800 --> 00:25:01.500
Then we're just left with ax
plus by plus cz equals 0.

00:25:02.180 --> 00:25:05.520
But if you just look at this, it
looks like the dot product of x,

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:08.520
y, z with a, b, c.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:08.940
Interesting.

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:09.600
OK.

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:10.600
Yeah.

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:14.160
So this is saying all vectors x,
y, z,

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.720
like if x, y, and z are the
components of the vector,

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:19.300
if that vector satisfies this
equation, well then it's

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:22.660
orthogonal to a, b, c.

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:25.460
That would force it to be on
that plane because if not, it

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:26.760
wouldn't be orthogonal.
Exactly.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:27.760
Exactly.

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:30.060
Yeah.

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:30.740
Good.
Paul.

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:31.820
I think I have two questions.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:32.700
Sure.

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:35.000
So is the only time we would
ever really need to use the

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.560
cross product if the question
