of the other vectors that are
there.

00:56:05.220 --> 00:56:08.620
So this collection is not
linearly independent.

00:56:08.620 --> 00:56:10.680
What about the second
collection? Is it linearly

00:56:10.680 --> 00:56:11.680
independent?

00:56:12.940 --> 00:56:16.820
No, because I can make this
vector using some of the other

00:56:16.820 --> 00:56:17.800
vectors that are here.

00:56:17.800 --> 00:56:21.680
I can make this vector 0, 0, 0,
1 by taking 1, 1, 1, 1 and

00:56:21.680 --> 00:56:23.580
subtracting this and then
subtracting this.

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:25.920
And I'd be left with 0, 0, 0, 1.

00:56:25.920 --> 00:56:29.670
So this collection is not
linearly independent.

00:56:36.340 --> 00:56:40.160
There's another equivalent
definition that is slightly more

00:56:40.160 --> 00:56:42.820
formal mathematically that we
will use in proofs often,

00:56:42.820 --> 00:56:43.840
because this one,

00:56:43.840 --> 00:56:46.020
none of the vectors can be
written as linear combinations

00:56:46.020 --> 00:56:47.980
of the others.
That's the important definition

00:56:47.980 --> 00:56:50.600
I want you to have in your mind,
but it's hard to write that down

00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:51.600
algebraically.

00:56:52.040 --> 00:56:54.290
There's an equivalent
definition.

00:56:56.360 --> 00:57:00.260
It's that vectors are linearly
independent if the only solution

00:57:00.260 --> 00:57:04.140
to

00:57:04.140 --> 00:57:08.780
a1 times v1,

00:57:09.340 --> 00:57:11.320
see if you can predict where
this is going,

00:57:11.320 --> 00:57:14.320
plus a2 times v2,

00:57:16.580 --> 00:57:21.500
all the way through ad times vd,

00:57:21.500 --> 00:57:26.700
equals the zero vector is where
all of the coefficients

00:57:26.700 --> 00:57:30.500
are zero.

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:42.260
So there's two equivalent
definitions of a collection of

00:57:42.260 --> 00:57:43.700
vectors being linearly
independent.

00:57:43.700 --> 00:57:46.420
The first definition is this
collection of vectors is

00:57:46.420 --> 00:57:50.360
linearly independent if every
single one of them bring

00:57:50.360 --> 00:57:53.140
something new to the table.
You can't make any of them using

00:57:53.140 --> 00:57:54.640
the others.

00:57:55.180 --> 00:57:57.120
It turns out, and I'm going to
give you some intuition for this

00:57:57.120 --> 00:57:57.980
in just a second,

00:57:57.980 --> 00:58:02.180
that is the same as saying the
only way you can make the zero

00:58:02.180 --> 00:58:03.820
vector using them

00:58:03.820 --> 00:58:06.060
is by taking zero of each one.

00:58:06.060 --> 00:58:09.440
There's no other way to make the
zero vector.

00:58:09.440 --> 00:58:12.840
These are the same definition.
And

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:16.520
I'll say otherwise,

00:58:20.000 --> 00:58:24.960
they are linearly

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:26.660
dependent.

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:32.890
OK.

00:58:33.680 --> 00:58:37.330
So why are these definitions
related?

00:58:37.330 --> 00:58:39.180
Let's come up with an example.
Well,

00:58:39.180 --> 00:58:40.160
let's suppose

00:58:40.160 --> 00:58:51.600
we

00:58:51.600 --> 00:58:56.160
have three vectors, v1, v2, and
v3.

00:58:56.640 --> 00:58:59.660
Suppose they all just live in
Rn.

00:58:59.660 --> 00:59:02.660
Doesn't matter what universe
they live in.

00:59:03.400 --> 00:59:07.900
And that v3 is a linear
combination of the first two.

00:59:08.620 --> 00:59:11.600
2v1 plus 7v2.

00:59:13.940 --> 00:59:16.940
Are these vectors linearly
independent?

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:28.320
Is this collection of vectors
linearly independent?

00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:30.040
No, you're shaking your head no.
Why?

00:59:30.040 --> 00:59:32.800
Because v3 is, what's it called?

00:59:32.800 --> 00:59:37.300
It's made by v1 and v2.

00:59:41.260 --> 00:59:42.260
Good.

00:59:49.820 --> 00:59:54.480
Note that for them to be
linearly independent, the

00:59:54.480 --> 00:59:57.420
requirement is that none of them
can be written as linear

00:59:57.420 --> 00:59:59.120
combinations of the others.

00:59:59.120 --> 01:00:02.340
The opposite of none is at least
one.

01:00:02.340 --> 01:00:04.920
It doesn't mean that every one
of them needs to be made as

01:00:04.920 --> 01:00:07.540
linear combinations of the
others, but because at least

01:00:07.540 --> 01:00:08.700
one,

01:00:13.060 --> 01:00:16.810
LC just means linear combination
of others.

01:00:17.620 --> 01:00:22.180
Why is that? Well, it's because
we told you v3 is equal to 2v1

01:00:22.180 --> 01:00:23.680
plus 7v2.

01:00:24.260 --> 01:00:26.760
But it turns out with this
equation, you can actually make

01:00:26.760 --> 01:00:28.280
all three vectors using the
others.

01:00:28.280 --> 01:00:30.700
You can just rearrange it.
So for example,

01:00:30.700 --> 01:00:35.950
can I make v1 using the other
two?

01:00:36.720 --> 01:00:38.180
Sure, you can.
The numbers are just a little

01:00:38.180 --> 01:00:39.100
ugly.

01:00:39.100 --> 01:00:43.980
But you solve, you get 2v1
equals negative 7v2 plus v3.

01:00:44.600 --> 01:00:47.940
What that means is v1, if you
divide both sides by 2 here,

01:00:47.940 --> 01:00:53.190
is negative 7 over 2v2 plus 1
half times v3.

01:00:53.440 --> 01:00:54.220
So v3,

01:00:54.220 --> 01:00:55.320
there's nothing special about
it.

01:00:55.320 --> 01:00:58.340
It's just the one I told you the
most direct equation about.

01:00:58.340 --> 01:01:00.400
Either way, these are all
telling us the same thing.

01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:04.150
This collection of vectors is
not linearly independent.

01:01:06.020 --> 01:01:08.140
By the way, hopefully it's
clear.

01:01:08.140 --> 01:01:11.020
The property of linear
independence is a property of a

01:01:11.020 --> 01:01:13.080
collection of vectors.
If I just write down the vector

01:01:13.080 --> 01:01:16.600
1, 0 and ask, is it linearly
independent, well,

01:01:16.600 --> 01:01:20.280
that's not a question that is
defined unless I have a

01:01:20.280 --> 01:01:21.580
collection of vectors.

01:01:21.580 --> 01:01:24.400
Now, maybe my collection is just
the vector 1, 0.

01:01:24.400 --> 01:01:26.400
And is that collection linearly
independent?

01:01:26.400 --> 01:01:29.420
Sure, because there's no other
vector to make 1, 0 with.

01:01:29.420 --> 01:01:32.340
But a vector on its own,

01:01:32.340 --> 01:01:34.520
whether it's linearly
independent or not, that's not a

01:01:34.520 --> 01:01:37.060
question you can ask.
The property is of a collection

01:01:37.060 --> 01:01:38.560
of vectors.

01:01:39.360 --> 01:01:44.160
But my point here was to give
you some intuition for this

01:01:44.160 --> 01:01:46.240
second definition of linear
independence.

01:01:46.240 --> 01:01:49.940
We know why this collection, v1,
v2, v3, is not linearly

01:01:49.940 --> 01:01:50.940
independent.

01:01:51.860 --> 01:01:56.240
Because I can make at least one
of them using the others.

01:01:56.240 --> 01:01:58.460
What was that second definition
about, and how is that related

01:01:58.460 --> 01:01:59.960
to this?

01:02:05.220 --> 01:02:06.220
Allison?

01:02:07.320 --> 01:02:11.040
The second definition is that we
could only make the zero vector

01:02:11.040 --> 01:02:15.540
if we just made all the
coefficients zero.

01:02:15.760 --> 01:02:16.440
Good.

01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:19.180
Remember, the second definition
of linear independence is we say

01:02:19.180 --> 01:02:22.020
this collection of vectors is
linearly independent if the only

01:02:22.020 --> 01:02:25.620
way you can make the zero vector
using them is by taking zero of

01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:26.740
each one.

01:02:26.740 --> 01:02:28.960
Is that the case here?

01:02:28.960 --> 01:02:29.480
No,

01:02:29.480 --> 01:02:31.730
why not? Yeah.

01:02:33.260 --> 01:02:38.340
Can you think of another way to
make the zero vector using the

01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:42.260
three vectors v1, v2, and v3?

01:02:42.260 --> 01:02:42.800
Yeah.

01:02:42.800 --> 01:02:46.340
We need 2v1 plus 7v2 minus v3.

01:02:46.340 --> 01:02:47.340
Good.

01:02:54.980 --> 01:02:58.980
All this is saying is if at
least one of the vectors can be

01:02:58.980 --> 01:03:00.980
made with the others,

01:03:00.980 --> 01:03:04.060
then you can rearrange that
equation to make the zero vector

01:03:04.060 --> 01:03:07.800
using some of the vectors in a
non-trivial way.

01:03:07.800 --> 01:03:11.300
A trivial way would be zero
times each one.

01:03:11.300 --> 01:03:13.820
But here what we've seen is that
I was able to make the zero

01:03:13.820 --> 01:03:18.320
vector by taking at least one
non-zero scalar.

01:03:23.320 --> 01:03:26.860
So do we see this correspondence
between one of the vectors being

01:03:26.860 --> 01:03:31.300
redundant and being able to make
the zero vector in a non-trivial

01:03:31.300 --> 01:03:32.300
way?

01:03:32.540 --> 01:03:34.360
Do you see how those are kind of
related?

01:03:34.360 --> 01:03:35.860
OK, good.

01:03:58.140 --> 01:04:00.680
By the way, as you'll see in
tomorrow's lab, this second

01:04:00.680 --> 01:04:04.430
definition is called the formal
definition.

01:04:07.880 --> 01:04:11.940
And you'll do some proofs that
involve the formal definition.

01:04:11.940 --> 01:04:15.580
But I want you to have the
intuitive definition in mind.

01:04:15.580 --> 01:04:19.480
Vectors are linearly independent
if there's no way to make any

01:04:19.480 --> 01:04:23.320
one of them using the others.
Okay, and the like way that I

01:04:23.320 --> 01:04:26.000
think about this conceptually

01:04:26.000 --> 01:04:29.940
Is that the vectors are linearly
independent if every vector

01:04:29.940 --> 01:04:32.520
brings something new to the
table if you're considering

01:04:32.520 --> 01:04:33.860
their span

01:04:33.860 --> 01:04:37.020
Every vector there is necessary.
If you remove any one of them

01:04:37.020 --> 01:04:40.260
the span decreases it removes
some of the vectors that you can

01:04:40.260 --> 01:04:41.260
build

01:04:42.700 --> 01:04:43.700
Okay

01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:50.370
What questions do we have so far
about linear independence

01:04:59.780 --> 01:05:01.660
There's one thing in the notes I
want to point out, because

01:05:01.660 --> 01:05:03.060
you're going to do this

01:05:03.060 --> 01:05:06.720
in lab tomorrow and in

01:05:06.720 --> 01:05:12.520
homework this

01:05:12.520 --> 01:05:13.360
week.

01:05:13.360 --> 01:05:15.640
Two things I want to point out.
OK, actually.

01:05:15.640 --> 01:05:16.460
So first,

01:05:16.460 --> 01:05:19.300
one of the reasons we care about
vectors being linearly

01:05:19.300 --> 01:05:23.560
independent is that there's a
beautiful property of vectors

01:05:23.560 --> 01:05:25.120
that are linearly independent,

01:05:25.120 --> 01:05:27.100
which is that if you have
vectors that are linearly

01:05:27.100 --> 01:05:28.100
independent,

01:05:29.240 --> 01:05:32.360
then any of their linear
combinations

01:05:32.360 --> 01:05:35.220
can only be made in one way.

01:05:35.220 --> 01:05:37.820
I think we briefly talked about
this before.

01:05:37.820 --> 01:05:40.120
What that means is if I give you
a collection of building blocks

01:05:40.120 --> 01:05:41.600
that are these vectors

01:05:41.600 --> 01:05:44.100
v's, and I say they're linearly
independent, what that means is

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:47.480
any other vector I can make
using the building blocks,

01:05:47.480 --> 01:05:49.960
there's only one recipe to make
it.

01:05:49.960 --> 01:05:52.580
There's only one combination of
scalars that would allow you to

01:05:52.580 --> 01:05:54.830
make that output.

01:05:56.720 --> 01:05:59.720
It's like, for example,

01:06:03.460 --> 01:06:07.210
this is important enough to
write down.

01:06:29.380 --> 01:06:32.500
I'll give you an example here.

01:06:32.500 --> 01:06:37.300
Suppose I have the vector u
being 1, 0 and the vector v

01:06:37.300 --> 01:06:41.820
being 1, 1.
So let's consider the vector

01:06:41.820 --> 01:06:43.580
3,

01:06:43.900 --> 01:06:44.900
4.

01:06:46.720 --> 01:06:51.970
Firstly, do we agree that u and
v are linearly independent?

01:06:52.240 --> 01:06:57.480
All that's needed for them to be
linearly independent,

01:06:57.480 --> 01:07:00.840
since we're dealing with two
vectors in R2,

01:07:00.840 --> 01:07:03.220
is that they don't point in the
same direction.

01:07:03.220 --> 01:07:05.480
If they did point in the same
direction, then they'd be scalar

01:07:05.480 --> 01:07:07.380
multiples of each other, and you
can make one with the other.

01:07:07.380 --> 01:07:08.260
Okay,

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:11.560
notice it's not required that
they're orthogonal.

01:07:11.560 --> 01:07:14.220
Orthogonality is like a very
strict condition.

01:07:14.220 --> 01:07:17.340
Okay, it's like a even more
supercharged version of linear

01:07:17.340 --> 01:07:17.940
independence.

01:07:17.940 --> 01:07:20.600
If vectors are orthogonal, then
they're definitely linearly

01:07:20.600 --> 01:07:21.600
independent.

01:07:21.680 --> 01:07:24.180
But you don't need to be
orthogonal to be linearly

01:07:24.180 --> 01:07:24.740
independent.

01:07:24.740 --> 01:07:26.720
For example, this U and V are
linearly independent, but

01:07:26.720 --> 01:07:27.860
they're not orthogonal.

01:07:27.860 --> 01:07:31.400
Okay, so orthogonality is like a
stricter condition.

01:07:31.400 --> 01:07:33.720
So these vectors U and V,
they're linearly independent,

01:07:33.720 --> 01:07:37.380
which means you can't make
either one with the other.

01:07:37.380 --> 01:07:40.440
What that means is anything you
can make with u and v,

01:07:40.440 --> 01:07:42.360
there's only one way to make it,

01:07:42.360 --> 01:07:43.160
OK?

01:07:43.160 --> 01:07:45.680
So let's consider this vector 3,
4.

01:07:45.680 --> 01:07:47.940
How do you make it with u and v?

01:07:47.940 --> 01:07:49.700
Well, here's how I think about
it.

01:07:49.700 --> 01:07:53.000
Only v controls the bottom
component here.

01:07:53.000 --> 01:07:57.500
What that means is the number
you multiply v by has to be 4.

01:07:59.440 --> 01:08:00.840
Do you agree with that?

01:08:00.840 --> 01:08:03.180
There's no other way you're
going to get a 4 down here.

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:05.060
But that would make a 4 up here.

01:08:05.060 --> 01:08:08.060
We want a 3.
So what should I multiply u by?

01:08:08.200 --> 01:08:08.560
Yeah,

01:08:08.560 --> 01:08:10.060
negative 1.

01:08:14.240 --> 01:08:19.340
All I'm getting at is that there
is no other numbers you could

01:08:19.340 --> 01:08:22.540
multiply u and v by in a linear
combination that would give you

01:08:22.540 --> 01:08:23.540
3, 4.

01:08:23.540 --> 01:08:24.220
It can't be done.

01:08:24.220 --> 01:08:26.480
There's only one way to do it.

01:08:26.480 --> 01:08:30.980
But what if I instead have?

01:08:39.100 --> 01:08:39.900
U,

01:08:39.900 --> 01:08:45.160
v, and let's toss in a
troublemaker here,

01:08:46.400 --> 01:08:47.900
0, 1.

01:08:50.740 --> 01:08:55.400
It's still true that 3, 4 is
equal to negative 1u plus 4

01:08:55.400 --> 01:08:56.780
times v.

01:08:56.780 --> 01:08:59.820
But can you think of another
linear combination? And here,

01:08:59.820 --> 01:09:05.820
why don't I put a plus 0 w?

01:09:06.280 --> 01:09:08.540
But can you think of another
linear combination of these

01:09:08.540 --> 01:09:13.040
three vectors that also gives me
3, 4?

01:09:13.240 --> 01:09:14.160
Yeah?

01:09:14.160 --> 01:09:16.040
3u plus 4w.

01:09:16.040 --> 01:09:19.280
Yeah, I could do 3us,

01:09:19.280 --> 01:09:21.530
ignore v entirely,

01:09:21.640 --> 01:09:22.640
goodbye,

01:09:27.500 --> 01:09:31.250
And then take four w's.

01:09:31.360 --> 01:09:33.420
Can you think of a way to do
this

01:09:33.420 --> 01:09:36.980
in which none of the
coefficients are 0?

01:09:36.980 --> 01:09:37.960
Sure, why not?

01:09:37.960 --> 01:09:40.860
Just to illustrate how absurd we
can make this,

01:09:40.860 --> 01:09:44.820
why do we make it 2026v?

01:09:44.820 --> 01:09:48.620
What would you have to adjust
the numbers you multiply u and w

01:09:48.620 --> 01:09:49.620
by?

01:09:49.960 --> 01:09:54.460
What would the coefficient on u
need to be?

01:09:56.980 --> 01:09:57.740
Sorry.

01:09:57.740 --> 01:09:59.240
Negative 2023.

01:09:59.420 --> 01:10:01.670
Negative 2023, good.

01:10:09.520 --> 01:10:14.020
And then what would the number
on w need to be?

01:10:17.720 --> 01:10:22.780
Not quite, because here we're at
2026, 2026.

01:10:23.220 --> 01:10:28.480
We got rid of 2023 on the top,
but we still need to make the

01:10:28.480 --> 01:10:30.730
bottom a 4.

01:10:30.840 --> 01:10:33.280
So what should the bottom number
be?

01:10:33.280 --> 01:10:34.020
Good.

01:10:34.020 --> 01:10:35.520
Negative 2022.

01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:43.300
And hopefully it's clear that, I
mean, I picked 2023 or 2026

01:10:43.300 --> 01:10:44.800
because it's the current year.
But you could have picked any

01:10:44.800 --> 01:10:46.320
number you want.

01:10:46.320 --> 01:10:49.020
And you would have been able to
find a linear combination of u,

01:10:49.020 --> 01:10:51.900
v, and w that gives you the
vector 3, 4.

01:10:51.900 --> 01:10:55.320
And that's because these vectors
are not linearly independent.

01:10:55.320 --> 01:10:59.820
When you have vectors that are
not linearly independent,

01:11:05.620 --> 01:11:09.740
Their linear combinations are
not unique, okay? What that

01:11:09.740 --> 01:11:12.880
means is since I was able to
make 3, 4 and my building blocks

01:11:12.880 --> 01:11:15.600
are not linearly independent, I
can make 3, 4 infinitely many

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:16.600
ways.

01:11:16.680 --> 01:11:20.920
Here these vectors u and v are
linearly independent, so their

01:11:20.920 --> 01:11:23.920
linear combinations are unique.

01:11:28.280 --> 01:11:34.180
I'll say this is the only linear
combination

01:11:34.180 --> 01:11:38.060
of u and v that equals 3, 4.

01:11:38.060 --> 01:11:39.820
Are we clear on that idea?

01:11:39.820 --> 01:11:42.000
And if you want a bit more
intuition for where this is

01:11:42.000 --> 01:11:43.360
coming from,

01:11:43.360 --> 01:11:48.280
it comes from the fact that you
can make, say,

01:11:48.280 --> 01:11:50.260
v using u and w,

01:11:50.260 --> 01:11:53.780
because u plus w equals v.

01:11:53.780 --> 01:11:57.780
What that means is u minus v
plus w equals the 0 vector.

01:11:57.780 --> 01:11:59.720
What that means is if you just
start with this linear

01:11:59.720 --> 01:12:02.800
combination, you can add
whatever you want times the 0

01:12:02.800 --> 01:12:06.160
vector to both sides.
And you're just adding 0 to both

01:12:06.160 --> 01:12:08.540
sides, which is allowed.

01:12:08.540 --> 01:12:11.920
And you can multiply that
equation, u minus v plus w

01:12:11.920 --> 01:12:12.880
equals 0,

01:12:12.880 --> 01:12:14.520
by whatever you want.
Multiply it by pi, your

01:12:14.520 --> 01:12:15.580
birthday, whatever.

01:12:15.580 --> 01:12:18.280
And add it to this equation,
you'll a new linear combination

01:12:18.280 --> 01:12:21.040
of u, v, and w that gives you
the zero vector.

01:12:21.040 --> 01:12:22.540
How's that?

01:12:23.200 --> 01:12:26.380
So, I guess I'm just trying to
